fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1004", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A SCOPE STILL CARRIES NO TABLE, SO A CROSS-TABLE MINT THAT IS IN RANGE IS SILENT. WI-990 moved the mint onto the table (`SymbolTable.scopeOf`) so every site WRITES which table it means, and made it refuse an out-of-range symbol. That refusal is the wrong direction and its own doc says so: `fileSym` is small and `kb.symbols` large, so the loader's own hazard — a parse-time symbol minted as a KB scope, `ScopeId.of(imp.path.last)` in its old spelling — is always IN range and always passes. MEASURED and asserted as a limit in `ScopeIdentityTest` (`WI-990: the mint refuses a symbol its table never issued`): a second table's symbol #0 mints a scope that `scopeDisplayName` calls `_global`, while its own table calls it `a`. The check catches only the reverse direction, which is the one the WI-990 ticket happened to measure. WORK: make the cross-table hand-off a COMPILE error by making `ScopeId` a path-dependent member of `SymbolTable` — `class SymbolTable: opaque type ScopeId = TermSymbol`. Then `st1.ScopeId` and `st2.ScopeId` are distinct types and `st2.resolveInScope(n, aScopeOfSt1)` does not compile. VERIFIED against Scala 3.7.4 on a two-class scratch: it reports `Found: (s : a.ScopeId) Required: b.ScopeId`. NOTE this needs NO tag on `TermSymbol` — the first guess, recorded in WI-990's own `scopeOf` comment before the check, was that closing this meant tagging the symbol and so reaching the term store and the discrimination tree. It does not. WHAT MAKES IT INVASIVE, and it is the whole cost: three types hold a `ScopeId` with no table in scope — `SymbolDef.Resolved.scope` and `ScopeInclusion.parent` (both in `intern/Symbol.scala`, OUTSIDE the class) and `KnowledgeBase`'s `RuleEntry.domain` plus `byDomain_`'s key (WI-983). Each needs a type parameter or a move inside `SymbolTable`, and every loader signature threading a scope becomes a dependent method type (`def f(kb: KnowledgeBase, scope: kb.symbols.ScopeId)` — legal, parameters are stable identifiers). Settle FIRST whether `RuleEntry` gets a type param or the KB pins one table, because that decision sizes the rest. ACCEPTANCE: a `typeCheckErrors` case asserting that a scope minted by one `SymbolTable` is rejected at another's `resolveInScope`, with a positive control on the same-table call, as `ScopeIdentityTest` already does for the Int/TermId/companion-mint cases; the `WI-990: the mint refuses...` case's third assertion (the in-range survivor) must then be INVERTED, since it exists only to record this hole. TRAP, measured during WI-990: zinc does NOT recompile a `typeCheckErrors` test when the code it type-checks changes, so backing the change out and running `testOnly` reports GREEN off a stale class file — every control here must be measured after `core/clean`. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. RUSTLAND has no twin yet: WI-984 (open) has not brought `ScopeId` there at all, and rustland's scope keys are still TermId raws, so do not treat this as a parity item — note it in WI-984 instead. Found by code review of WI-990 (2026-08-04). Related: WI-990, WI-976, WI-983, WI-984."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T03:24:31Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1004", name: "scaland")
